192.168.198.2>ping6

NAME
     ping6 - send ICMPv6 ECHO_REQUEST packets to network hosts

SYNOPSIS
     ping6 [-silent] [-Tnrt] [-c count] [-h hoplimit] [-m ifname] [-s packetsize] [-w timeout]
           [-F label] [-S src_addr] [-Q tclass] [-V vr] [hop ...] host

DESCRIPTION
     ping6 uses the ICMPv6 protocol's mandatory ICMP6_ECHO_REQUEST datagram to
     elicit an ICMP6_ECHO_REPLY from a host or gateway.  ICMP6_ECHO_REQUEST
     datagrams (``pings'') have an IPv6 header, and ICMPv6 header formatted as
     documented in RFC2463. It is possible to specify additional nodes that the
     ping request must visit before reaching the final destination.
     This is accomplished by adding a routing header to the packet.

     -c count
             Stop after sending count ECHO_RESPONSE packets.
     -F label
             Specifcy the flow label in the IPv6 header.
     -h hoplimit
             Set the IPv6 hoplimit.
     -I ifname
             Forces the packet to be sent on the specified interface.
     -m <ifname>
             Name of outgoing interface for multicast addresses.
     -N addr Uses 'addr' as the first-hop node.
     -n      Numeric output only. No attempts will be made to look up
             names for host addresses.
     -Q tclass
             Specify the traffic class in the IPv6 header.
     -r      Bypass the normal routing tables and send directly to a host on
             an attached network.
     -S src_addr  Specifies the address to use a source address when sending.
     -s packetsize
             Specifies the number of data bytes to be sent. The default is
             56, which translates into 64 ICMP data bytes when combined with
             the 8 bytes of ICMP header data.
     -T Prefer temporary addresses as source.
     -V vr   Specify virtual router. vr = 0 if not specified.
     -X ID   Assign the ICMPv6 socket to stack instance ID.
     -w timeout  Timeout in milliseconds, default is 1000 ms.
     <hop [nexthop] [...]> Zero or more nodes that the echo request must pass,
             in order, before reaching the final host node
             Should set sysvar ifname.inet6.RtHdr0 to 1 to enable processing of IPv6 Routing Header on the nodes
     -silent no output, returns success or failure.
             MUST be first argument.

